    title: "Sharing Your Opinion Respectfully",
    theMoment: "You have a view that differs from the room — or from one person — and you want to say it without seeming combative or starting a fight.",
    whatsReallyGoingOn: "People don't bristle at different opinions — they bristle at feeling dismissed. You can disagree with almost anything if you first show you heard them and you frame it as your view rather than the verdict. \"I see it differently\" lands; \"you're wrong\" detonates. The respect is in the framing, not the silence.",
    whatToSay: ["\"That's a fair point — here's where I land a little differently.\"","\"I see why you'd say that. I actually lean the other way, and here's why.\"","\"For me, it comes down to…\"","\"My honest take is a bit different.\"","\"I might be wrong, but my instinct is…\"","\"We may just see this one differently, and that's okay.\""],
    questionsToAsk: ["\"Can I push back on that a little?\"","\"What's leading you to that?\"","\"Have you thought about it from this angle?\""],
    howToSayIt: "Lead with the acknowledgment so they don't feel steamrolled, then state your view plainly. Keep your tone curious, not corrective — you're offering a perspective, not issuing a ruling. Stay calm and unhurried; the calmer you are, the more respectful even a strong disagreement feels.",
