    title: "Difficult Family Conversations",
    theMoment: "There's a charged topic with family — old wounds, money, parenting differences, a relative who oversteps — and family history makes it ten times harder than the same conversation with anyone else.",
    whatsReallyGoingOn: "Family conversations carry extra weight because of history, roles, and the fact that you can't just walk away — old patterns reactivate fast. The move is to step out of the old role, stay calm even when they don't, and focus on what you need now rather than rehashing the whole past. You can change your part of a dynamic even when they won't change theirs.",
    whatToSay: ["\"I don't want to fall into our usual thing. Can we try this differently?\"","\"I'm not the kid in this dynamic anymore — I'd like to talk adult to adult.\"","\"I love you, and I need [X] to be different between us.\"","\"I'd rather have a real relationship than keep avoiding this.\"","\"I'm not going to argue about the past — I want to talk about now.\"","\"Let's not relitigate everything. What do we do going forward?\""],
    questionsToAsk: ["\"Can we talk about something without it turning into the usual?\"","\"What do you actually need from me here?\"","\"How do we want this to look going forward?\""],
    howToSayIt: "Stay calm even if they escalate — you holding steady is what breaks the old loop. Refuse the bait of relitigating ancient history; keep pulling it back to now. And lead with love and limits together, since with family the relationship and the boundary both matter.",
    commonMistakes: ["Slipping back into the childhood role.","Getting pulled into rehashing the entire past.","Matching their escalation.","Avoiding the topic forever to keep a fragile peace."],
    tryItThisWeek: "Approach one family topic from your adult self — calm, focused on now, love plus a limit.",
    keyLine: "\"I don't want to fall into our usual thing — can we try this differently?\"",
    note: "Some family situations involve genuine harm. Stepping back or seeking outside support is a valid choice, not a failure.",
