    title: "Repairing A Relationship",
    theMoment: "A relationship that matters to you has been damaged — by a fight, a drift, a betrayal, or just time — and you want to repair it but don't know how to make the first move.",
    whatsReallyGoingOn: "Most damaged relationships stay broken not because they can't heal, but because nobody makes the first move — both people wait, each assuming the other should reach out. Being the one to extend the olive branch isn't weakness; it's the only thing that actually starts repair. The reach matters more than who was \"right.\"",
    whatToSay: ["\"I've missed you, and I don't want to let this stay broken.\"","\"I've been thinking about us. Can we talk?\"","\"I know I played a part in this, and I'm sorry for my piece.\"","\"I don't want to keep score — I just want us back.\"","\"No pressure, but the door's open whenever you're ready.\"","\"I'd rather try and have it be awkward than lose this.\""],
    questionsToAsk: ["\"Is there room to start fresh?\"","\"What would it take to rebuild this?\"","\"Can we find our way back?\""],
    howToSayIt: "Reach out first and let go of who was right — repair starts with the extended hand, not the won argument. Own your part sincerely, even if it's only part. Keep the pressure low so they can come toward you; an open door invites better than a demand.",
    commonMistakes: ["Waiting for the other person to reach out first.","Making repair conditional on \"winning\" the original fight.","Owning nothing, or over-grovelling.","Letting pride keep a valuable relationship broken."],
    tryItThisWeek: "Reach out to one person you've been estranged from — first, and without keeping score.",
    keyLine: "\"I've missed you, and I don't want to let this stay broken.\"",
